There were two versions of a power-on board made in 2019. The schematics for the second version have been lost. The board was meant to do a number of things.
1) Implement a circuit suggested for the Kelly e-bike controller. The controller always receives 36V power and ground from the battery. When the switch is off, power goes though a precharging resistor, but the unit doesn't do anything. When the switch is turned on, a relay removes the resistor and sends a low current 36V signal to a separate line on the charger, which turns it on.
2) Implement a watchdog timer on am Arduino Nano. If the dog is not petted regularly, the DBW board has stopped working and a second relay will turn off the e-bike controller.
3) Read the Hall effect sensor from the wheel and compute the speed.

The Arduino Nano functions have been abandoned. The board has been cut down to only implement item (1). The only components present are:
Input connector X5: 36V from battery. ground, 36V to e-bike controller
R2: 1K 10W resistor
Relay: T9AS1D12-48
Diode D1 1N5400
The holes designed for an on-board switch have been replace by a connector to an  off-board switch.
The 2A fuse on the line that turns on the controller has been jumbered out. When power is turned on, 36V goes to a buck converter that converts it to 24V and routs it to DBW through a 10A fuse. There is an external 2A fuse on the 36V signal to turn on the controller.
